// app:headless — the same runtime with nothing on the other end of it.
//
//     import app "app:headless"
//     import "app:draw"
//
//     !void process(f32 dt): ...
//
//     !i32 main():
//         try app.init("my batch job")
//         app.hook(process: process)
//         try app.run() else code:
//             fmt.println("app.run failed: ", code)
//             return 1
//         return 0
//
// `main` is `!i32` because both `init` and `run` are failable — `init` refuses
// a frame budget that is not one, and `run` carries out a failure raised inside
// the frame loop, which is where a hook's own `fail` is raised.
//
// ## The facade is the front door, and the body is the other facade's
//
// A program used to bind this package under a name of its own, reach into it
// for one line, and write everything else against `app:app`. It does not any
// more: this package carries the surface a program writes — `init`, `hook`,
// `run`, `quit`, `canvas`, the two allocators, the four `on_*` setters — as
// one-line forwards to `app:app`, and a program binds **it** as `app`. The
// argument for the alias, and why `app:app` could not have grown the call
// instead (it would be `ERR0302`, a cycle), is written out in `app:graphical`'s
// package comment and is the same argument here.
//
// **`hook`, `run`, `quit`, `canvas` and `allocator` match `app:graphical`'s
// exactly**, which is the point of doing this in both places: the *body* of a
// program is identical under either facade, and swapping
// `import app "app:headless"` for `import app "app:graphical"` moves nothing
// but the `init` line.
//
// **`init` is where the two honestly differ**, and it differs rather than being
// bent into a match. `app:graphical.init(w, h, title)` takes a window size
// because it opens a window; this one takes `init(name, max_frames = 0)`
// because a headless program has no window to size and does have a frame budget
// to set. A facade whose `init` pretended to take a width would be a facade
// lying about what it is, which is the exact failure `app:graphical` spent a
// milestone committing with its capability set.
//
// No window, no terminal, no input source, no GPU. It installs a `Backend` on
// the app singleton whose `run` owns a plain frame loop: `app.startup()`, then
// `app.tick(dt)` with a real monotonic `dt` until `app.quitting()`, then
// `app.teardown()`. That is the whole of the loop.
//
// ## It brings `app:draw` up too, in the mode that paints nothing
//
// This is the decision in this package most worth arguing, because it is not
// free.
//
// **What it does.** `surface_up` calls `draw.init_headless()` and makes a root
// canvas, exactly as `app:graphical` does with `draw.init()`. `render` calls
// `draw.canvas_render` on it every frame. `app:draw`'s headless server walks
// the tree, orders it, resolves clips and tints and counts every command and
// primitive a GPU render would have issued — and calls not one sokol function.
// So `app.canvas()` answers a real canvas under this facade and a program's
// recording code runs unchanged.
//
// **Why.** The two facades exist so that a program's body is portable between
// them, and a canvas that exists under one and not the other breaks exactly
// that promise at the one moment it is most likely to be used: moving a sketch
// from a batch check to a window, or the reverse — running a graphical
// program's logic under a gate with no display. `app:draw`'s counting server
// was built for that second case; leaving it out of the facade whose whole job
// is *no display* would be leaving it where nobody can reach it.
//
// **What it costs, in numbers.** `app:draw` imports `vendor:sokol/sokol_gp` and
// `vendor:sokol/sokol_gfx`, so a headless program links them whether or not it
// ever calls one. Measured on `example/log`, the same program built before and
// after this change: **42 104 → 304 488 bytes** of binary, **222 209 → 953 166
// bytes** of emitted C, and **3.0 s → 6.3 s** of build. That is a sevenfold
// binary and a doubled build for a program that prints numbers, and it is the
// honest price of the portability above.
//
// **The escape is real and it is one line.** A program that must not link sokol
// writes `import "app:app"` and installs a `Backend` of its own with the three
// surface slots left nil; it gets the loop, both allocators, the job pool and
// the whole frame order, and `app:app` imports no drawing at all. That escape
// is *why* the services are vtable slots rather than calls inside the core —
// see `app:app`'s vtable section.
//
// **It does not bring `app:font` up, and neither facade does.** A font is a
// face at a size and the size is the program's; the argument, and what a
// runtime-owned default face would have cost every program in bytes, is at
// `app:graphical`'s `canvas()`.
//
// **It still does not advertise `VectorDraw`.** A capability is a promise about
// what reaches a screen, and nothing here does. `draw.headless()` is how a
// program asks whether it is recording into the counting server, and that is a
// question about `app:draw` rather than about this backend.
//
// ## Why it exists
//
// The terminal backend used to be the only working one, so every headless
// program in the tree — a job-pool demo, a scene-graph demo, a network server —
// imported a *terminal* package to get a frame loop, and then spent a branch on
// "am I actually attached to a tty" to decide whether to paint. That is a loop
// driver wearing a terminal's clothes. This package is the loop driver with the
// clothes taken off: a program that never draws now says so in its imports, and
// the graphical backend is free to be the only one that owns a screen.
//
// **It advertises `Threads` and nothing else, and that is the honest set.**
// `app:app`'s scheduler is real here — `core:jobs` starts a worker pool when
// the backend says `Threads`, and it does not care that nobody is watching.
// Everything else in `app.Capability` needs a substrate this package refuses to
// have: there is no event source to fill `app:input`'s queue (`Input`), nothing
// that reaches a screen (`VectorDraw`), no window (`Windowing`), no device
// (`Audio`) and no selection to read (`Clipboard`). A program that reaches for
// one of those gets the empty answer — `input.poll` returns 0 — and
// `app.supports` is how it asks in advance.
//
// **Termination is the caller's, with two backstops.** The loop runs until
// `app.quit()`; a program that means to stop calls it from `ready` or
// `process`. Because a headless program has nobody to notice it wedged, `init`
// also takes an optional frame budget: `init("x", 300)` stops the loop after
// 300 ticks whatever the program does, and `hit_max_frames()` afterwards says
// whether that is what happened — so a stuck example fails a gate run in
// bounded time instead of hanging it. `0` (the default) means "no cap". The
// second backstop is the runtime's: a hook that fails stops the loop too, and
// `run` reports its code.
package headless

import "app:app"
import "app:draw"
import "app:input"
import "core:time"

// Target milliseconds per frame. The terminal backend's loops both napped 16ms
// between ticks so an idle program did not spin a core at 100%, and so did the
// frame budgets written against them (`example/net`'s comments still quote
// "~16ms frame"). Kept, and made settable: a test that wants a thousand frames
// as fast as the machine will give them sets it to 0.
private i32 g_frame_ms = 16

// The frame budget (0 = none) and how the loop is going against it.
private i32  g_max_frames
private i32  g_frames
private bool g_hit_max

// The root canvas, and the size the counting render is measured against.
private draw.Canvas g_canvas = draw.NIL_CANVAS
private i32 g_surface_w = 1280
private i32 g_surface_h = 720

// ── failure codes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Three, and each is one thing this facade can actually see. A failure that
// reaches an `!i32 main()` unhandled **becomes the process exit status**, so
// these are small, fixed and named. `1` is left alone: it is what a `main`
// conventionally returns when its own assertions failed, and a runtime's
// refusal should not be confusable with the program's own verdict.
//
// **The numbers match `app:graphical`'s deliberately.** `2` is that facade's
// own `init` condition and this one's, `3` is *the drawing surface refused to
// come up* in both and `4` is *the root canvas refused to allocate* in both, so
// one gate script reads an exit status from either facade with one table.
const i32 E_FRAME_BUDGET = 2
const i32 E_DRAW_UP      = 3
const i32 E_ROOT_CANVAS  = 4

// Install the headless backend on the app singleton. This is the first line of
// a headless program.
//
// `name` is accepted for symmetry with `graphical.init(w, h, title)` and is not
// used for anything — there is no title bar to put it in. `max_frames` is the
// backstop described in the package doc comment: 0 for a loop that runs until
// `app.quit()`, a positive count to stop unconditionally after that many ticks.
//
// ## What this can fail with
//
// **One reportable failure, for the same reason `app:graphical.init` has one:
// it is the only thing this call can actually see.** There is no substrate to
// refuse anything — the whole of it is four stores and a `set_backend`. What is
// checkable is the budget: `E_FRAME_BUDGET` for a negative `max_frames`, which
// is neither of the two things the parameter means. `0` is "no cap" and a
// positive count is a count; a negative one would silently behave as "no cap"
// (`g_max_frames > 0` is what `run_loop` asks) and hang a program that thought
// it had set a deadline. The number is almost always computed —
// `example/graph` passes its own `FRAMES` — so a computation that went negative
// is exactly the case worth catching.
//
// **Bringing `app:draw` up is not on this list**, the same as in
// `app:graphical` and for a related reason: the surface comes up inside the
// loop, in `_surface_up`, so that both facades bring it up at the same point of
// the same lifecycle and a program cannot tell the two apart by when its canvas
// starts existing. Its refusal is `E_DRAW_UP` out of `run`.
!void init(string8 name, i32 max_frames = 0):
    if max_frames < 0:
        fail E_FRAME_BUDGET
    install_backend(name, max_frames)

// Install the headless backend on the app singleton.
//
// **Kept, unchanged, and not deprecated** — `init` is this call with the budget
// check and the `!`. It buys less than it used to: `run` is failable now
// whatever this is called, so a program that writes `new` still needs an
// `!i32 main()`. The two share `install_backend` rather than one calling the
// other, because a bare `new(...)` written inside package `headless` is
// `ERR0805` for one milestone longer; that is the same reason this package's
// own test installs through a private of its own.
void new(string8 name, i32 max_frames = 0):
    install_backend(name, max_frames)

// The `Backend` literal is written out one slot per line with the slot's name
// above it, because seven of the ten slots are `void()` and therefore one type —
// a swapped pair would compile. See `app:app`'s vtable section.
private void install_backend(string8 name, i32 max_frames):
    _ = name
    g_max_frames = max_frames
    g_frames = 0
    g_hit_max = false
    app.set_backend((nil,                      // data
                     run_loop,                 // run
                     begin_frame,              // begin_frame
                     end_frame,                // end_frame
                     app.caps(app.Capability.Threads),
                     _surface_up,              // surface_up
                     _surface_down,            // surface_down
                     _render,                  // render
                     _end_input,               // end_input
                     _deliver))                // deliver

// ── the root canvas ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The runtime's root canvas. Valid from the first line of `ready` until
// `shutdown` returns, and `draw.NIL_CANVAS` outside that window.
//
// It is `app:draw`'s counting server here rather than its GPU one — see the
// package comment — so recording into it and rendering it costs exactly the
// CPU work a windowed program would do and issues nothing. `draw.commands()`,
// `draw.primitives()` and `draw.state_changes()` after a frame answer what that
// frame would have cost a GPU.
//
// **A program may still call `draw.init` itself and should not**, on the same
// terms as under `app:graphical`: it is not refused, a second `init` destroys
// every canvas including this one, and `_render` detects the stale handle and
// `#panic`s by name on the first frame after rather than counting nothing for
// ever in silence.
draw.Canvas canvas():
    return g_canvas

// The size the counting render measures against, in pixels. There is no window
// to ask, so it is a number this package holds; `1280x720` by default.
//
// It is not decoration. `draw.canvas_render`'s `fb_w`/`fb_h` build the root
// clip rectangle every item's own rectangle is met against, so a program
// checking under this facade what a 1920x1080 window would have drawn sets this
// and gets the clipping that window would have applied. A program that never
// clips will not notice it exists.
void set_surface_size(i32 width, i32 height):
    g_surface_w = width
    g_surface_h = height

// ── the forwarding surface ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Everything below is `app:app` reached through this package, so that a program
// binding this one as `app` never has to import the core. Each is one line and
// adds no behaviour. It is `app:graphical`'s forwarding surface name for name
// and signature for signature — that is what makes a program's body portable
// between the two — minus the window knobs a headless program has no substrate
// for, and minus the five names an alias cannot carry (`app.Capability`,
// `app.JobArgs`, `app.JobFn`, `app.Handle`, `ROLE`), which `app:graphical`'s
// package comment lists and explains.

// The "omitted" sentinels this package's `hook` compares against.
//
// **This is the one forward in the file that is not one line**, for the two
// reasons `app:graphical` writes out at the same place: `app:app`'s own
// sentinels are `private`, deliberately, so nothing outside that file can hand
// in the value that means *unset*; and a default naming another package's
// function is order-dependent anyway.
//
// **This package is where the second one was measured.** Writing
// `= app.nothing` on these four parameters compiles from `example/log` and is
// refused the moment this package is the compilation root, which its own
// `@test` file makes it: `error[ERR0408]: 'app' is not a value in scope`, four
// times, on the defaults. That is the cross-package import-order defect
// reaching a default rather than a type — its root-package case is *"nothing
// can precede the root's own declarations"*. A surface that is legal until somebody adds a test is not a
// surface, so the sentinels are local and `hook` sets through `app.on_*`.
private !void nothing():               return
private !void nothing_process(f32 dt): _ = dt

// Assign any subset of the four lifecycle hooks in one call. An omitted
// parameter leaves that hook alone.
//
// **Write it all-named** — `app.hook(ready: ready, process: process,
// shutdown: shutdown)`. Three of the four parameters are the same type
// (`!void()`), so a positional call cannot be checked and a swap of two of them
// compiles silently; the argument and the rejected `distinct` alternative are
// written out in `app:app`'s own `hook` section, and every word of it applies
// here.
//
// **All four hooks are failable** (`!void()`, `!void(f32)`), and a `void()`
// function handed to one is `ERR0504` on this line naming both shapes. The
// repair is a `!` on the hook's return type.
//
// **`draw` is in this list and it is not decoration.** This backend's
// `begin_frame` and `end_frame` are both `return`, but `render` is not: a draw
// hook set on a headless app records into the same root canvas a windowed one
// would, and the counting render walks what it recorded. Keeping the parameter
// is what makes this signature identical to `app:graphical`'s — a program keeps
// its `hook` line verbatim when it swaps facades.
void hook(app.ReadyFn    ready    = nothing,
          app.ProcessFn  process  = nothing_process,
          app.DrawFn     draw     = nothing,
          app.ShutdownFn shutdown = nothing):
    if ready != nothing:
        app.on_ready(ready)
    if process != nothing_process:
        app.on_process(process)
    if draw != nothing:
        app.on_draw(draw)
    if shutdown != nothing:
        app.on_shutdown(shutdown)

// The four hooks, one at a time. Unchanged in meaning; see `app:app`.
void on_ready(app.ReadyFn f):       app.on_ready(f)
void on_process(app.ProcessFn f):   app.on_process(f)
void on_draw(app.DrawFn f):         app.on_draw(f)
void on_shutdown(app.ShutdownFn f): app.on_shutdown(f)

// Run until the app quits, a hook fails, or the frame budget runs out — then
// report whatever went wrong inside the loop.
//
// ## Failable now, and the old argument for `void` is what made it so
//
// This was `void`, and the argument written here was *"`app.run` `#panic`s on
// two conditions and neither can arise through this door … a `?` here would be
// a mark on a function with nothing to report, which costs every caller a `try`
// for a code none of them can ever see."* Correct then, and it is precisely why
// the `!` is here now: there **is** something to report. A hook may fail, and
// `_surface_up` may fail, and both happen inside the backend's own loop where
// nothing can unwind past — so the code is recorded and re-raised here, by the
// one call still on the stack when the loop returns. `app:app`'s frame-steps
// section writes the mechanism out.
//
// **The cost the old comment priced is now paid and it is five call sites.**
// `example/log`, `example/net` (twice), `example/graph` and `example/parjobs`
// all called this bare, and each becomes `try app.run() else code:` — which is
// four programs edited to gain an ending they previously had no way to express
// at all. Both facades' `run` still match, and what they match on is `!void`.
!void run():
    try app.run()

// Ask the loop to stop after this frame.
void quit():        app.quit()

// Whether something has asked the loop to stop — the program with `quit()`, or
// the runtime because a hook failed.
bool quitting():    return app.quitting()

// The two allocators, and the temp arena's checkpoints.
//
// **A program usually writes none of these.** Which arena is ambient depends on
// which hook is running: `ready` and `shutdown` run under `allocator()`, the
// persistent one, and `process` and `draw` run under `temp()`, which is rolled
// back at the end of every frame. So a bare `#make` lands in the right place in
// all four, and these names are for the two cases that are not the default —
// something allocated in `process` that has to outlive the frame
// (`using app.allocator():`), and a big transient that should not raise the temp
// arena's high-water mark for the rest of the run (`using app.enter_temp():` …
// `app.close_temp()`).
//
// `app:app`'s allocator and temp sections carry the whole argument, the
// dangling-reference hazard the arrangement accepts, and why this is the app's
// own temp arena rather than `core:runtime`'s.
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) allocator(): return app.allocator()
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) temp():      return app.temp()

// Open a temp frame and install it; close the innermost one.
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) enter_temp(bool escaping = false): return app.enter_temp(escaping)
bool close_temp():                           return app.close_temp()

// How many bytes the temp arena is holding (`0` at the top of every frame), and
// how many temp frames are open (`0` everywhere a correct program looks).
u64 temp_used():   return app.temp_used()
i32 temp_depth():  return app.temp_depth()

// Swap the persistent allocator. **Before `run`** — it refuses afterwards, and
// `app:app`'s own declaration says why a mid-run swap is not a thing that can
// quietly work now that the runtime installs the ambient.
void set_allocator((rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) a): app.set_allocator(a)

// The conditional-frame knobs (E7). Forwarded so a program's body stays
// portable: with no pass to skip and no swap to pace it, turning this on here
// only stops `tick` calling the draw hook and the counting render.
void request_redraw():                  app.request_redraw()
void set_redraw_on_demand(bool on):     app.set_redraw_on_demand(on)
bool redraw_pending():                  return app.redraw_pending()

// The program's own state pointer, for a library that has to reach it through
// the app rather than through package scope.
void set_user(rawptr p): app.set_user(p)
rawptr user():           return app.user()

// Milliseconds this backend aims to spend on a frame. 0 runs flat out.
void set_frame_time(i32 milliseconds):
    g_frame_ms = milliseconds

// Frames ticked so far — readable from inside `process`, and after `run`
// returns.
i32 frames():
    return g_frames

// True if the loop stopped because it ran out of frame budget with the
// program still going. False if the program called `app.quit()` — including
// on the very frame the budget ran out, which is what a program that passes
// its own frame count as the budget does every time — and false if a hook
// failed, which `run`'s own failure is what reports. For a program whose
// budget is a deadline rather than a plan this is the difference between
// "done" and "gave up", and it is the caller's to report: a backend does not
// print.
bool hit_max_frames():
    return g_hit_max

// ── backend vtable implementation ───────────────────────────────────────────

private void run_loop():
    app.startup()
    f64 last = time.now()
    bool spent = false
    for !app.quitting() && !spent:
        if g_frame_ms > 0:
            time.nap(g_frame_ms)
        f64 nowt = time.now()
        f32 dt = f32(nowt - last)
        last = nowt
        g_frames = g_frames + 1
        app.tick(dt)
        if g_max_frames > 0 && g_frames >= g_max_frames:
            // The budget ends the loop through this local, **not** through
            // `app.quit()`, and the difference is visible: `app:app`'s
            // quitting flag is set-only (there is no `unquit`), so a backend
            // that spent it on its own backstop would leave the singleton
            // permanently quitting — which a second `run` in one process, this
            // package's own test included, would then never enter. The
            // program's `quit()` is the program's to spend; a budget stop is
            // the backend's decision about its own loop and is recorded as
            // `hit_max_frames()` instead. A hook's failure is the *runtime's*
            // decision about the same loop and is recorded the third way, in
            // `app:app`'s own flag, which `run` clears — three endings, three
            // records, and `hit_max_frames()` answers about exactly one of
            // them.
            // `!app.quitting()` and not a bare `true`: a program whose own
            // stopping condition lands on the same frame as the budget (the
            // common case — `example/graph` passes its own `FRAMES` as the
            // budget, so the two agree by construction) finished, it did not
            // run out. The flag is for the caller that wants to tell those
            // endings apart, so it has to actually tell them apart.
            g_hit_max = !app.quitting()
            spent = true
    app.teardown()

private void begin_frame():
    return

private void end_frame():
    return

// ── the five service slots ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Bring `app:draw`'s counting server up and make the root canvas.
//
// **Called from inside `app.startup`, which opens `using app.allocator()`
// around everything it calls**, so `draw.init_headless()` — which takes no
// allocator and allocates to `#default` — puts its pools in the runtime's
// persistent allocator with no allocator written on either side of the call.
// That is the allocator rule — *don't thread allocators as parameters; change
// the ambient with `using`* — paying out across a package boundary.
//
// `init_headless` answers false only on an allocation refusal, there being no
// painter to refuse to start; `draw.last_error()` says so in words.
private !void _surface_up():
    if !draw.init_headless():
        fail E_DRAW_UP
    g_canvas = draw.canvas_create()
    if !draw.canvas_valid(g_canvas):
        fail E_ROOT_CANVAS

// Tear it down. Called after the program's `shutdown` hook, so a program's own
// release runs while its canvas is still valid.
private void _surface_down():
    draw.shutdown()
    g_canvas = draw.NIL_CANVAS

// Walk and count the root canvas. There is no pass to be inside — this
// backend's `begin_frame`/`end_frame` are both `return` — and the counting
// server calls no sokol function, so the frame's cost is CPU and the numbers
// `draw.commands()` and `draw.primitives()` answer afterwards are exact.
//
// The `canvas_valid` check is the detector described at `canvas()`: true on
// every frame of every correct program, false only when something outside the
// runtime tore `app:draw` down.
private void _render():
    if !draw.canvas_valid(g_canvas):
        #panic("app:headless: the runtime's root canvas is gone — something called draw.init() or draw.shutdown() behind the runtime's back. Record into app.canvas() instead, or drive app:draw yourself without this facade.")
    draw.canvas_render(g_canvas, g_surface_w, g_surface_h)

// End the input frame, on every frame, at the far end of it.
//
// **There is no event source here and the call is still made.** It costs two
// loops over `input.KEY_WORDS` and clears bits nothing ever set — and what it
// buys is that *"the runtime clears the edges, you never call `clear_edges`"*
// is one sentence rather than one sentence and a footnote about which facade.
// A program that pushes into `app:input` itself (a replay, a test harness, a
// synthetic input source) gets the same frame boundary it would get under a
// window, which is the only way that program could have been portable.
private void _end_input():
    input.clear_edges()

// Deliver what was queued for this frame, first in `app.tick`, before
// `process`. For the reason `_end_input` gives: a program that pushes into
// `app:input` itself gets the frame it would get under a window.
private void _deliver():
    input.deliver()
